pub fn copy -> CorrectionCopy = CorrectionCopy {
  summary: "Find the value stored under a key. Just contains a present value; Nothing means that the key is absent.",
  taskTitle: "Read a name by its key",
  purpose: "A Map associates keys with values. Here, \"name\" stores \"Aki\" and \"empty\" stores an empty string. Look up each key, then produce text for both a present value and an absent key.",
  setup: "import * as maps gives std/map the local name maps. Arguments follow the function name, separated by spaces: maps.get \"name\" names passes a key and then a Map. let names the Map returned by insert; insert does not change its input.",
  codeTitle: "Complete program: present, empty text, and absent keys",
  outputTitle: "Output",
  after: [
    "Maybe<String> has two forms: Just text contains a string, while Nothing has no value. match selects the matching form; Just text names the contained value text. The backtick string inserts text at ${text}, so a present empty string produces found: [].",
    "maps.singleton creates the first entry. maps.empty<String, String> () creates an empty Map with string keys and string values. The type arguments supply information that no entries can provide; () is the Unit argument, meaning no additional input. pub effect fn main is the program entry, do orders its output steps, and println prints each result."
  ],
  comparisonTitle: "The same lookup in TypeScript",
  comparisonBefore: "For Map<string, string>, JavaScript Map.get returns a string or undefined. An explicit undefined check preserves the empty string. Testing if (value) would confuse a present empty string with absence.",
  comparisonAfter: "Both examples distinguish absent keys from empty text. If a TypeScript Map also allows undefined as a stored value, use has to distinguish that value from absence. Seseragi expresses absence separately with Nothing.",
  detailTitle: "Key equality and the result type",
  details: [
    "K and V name the key and value types. Eq<K> means the key type supplies equality; Hash<K> means it supplies a hash used to locate candidate keys. Equal keys must have compatible hashes. The standard String implementations support the keys in this example.",
    "get returns Maybe<V> without changing the Map. The key is not an integer position: an Int key would still be a key. Use containsKey when you need only a presence check."
  ]
